      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535/assets/888a2f1f537f7381379a6f85dc850f29b42193eb53938832be8ede47fbe8264a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $4{x}^{2}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4{x}^{2}.\n$$",
