  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535",
    "id": "q-122339",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122339",
      "knowledge_point_id": "1320d414-11eb-58a9-b59f-a6f760e87578",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving a Differential Equation That Relates the Velocity and Displacement of a Particle",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of decreasing $x$ where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,m,$ its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has a displacement of $0\\,m$ when its velocity is $-2\\,m/s,$ find $v$ in terms of $x.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535/assets/ba6a85da04d9ba03bcdba9b938dac208a770933310cd25c19bf0ec3e19ef17c7.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{1}{16}x\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{1}{16}x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (\\frac{1}{16}x)\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=-2\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $C$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+C \\\\ C & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{32}{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+4 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{2}+64) \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the particle is moving in the direction of decreasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)<0,$ so we can discard the positive square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+64}.\n$$",
