  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535",
    "id": "q-122351",
    "status": "supported",
    "reason": null,
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122351",
      "knowledge_point_id": "550bb33b-c427-5667-977d-56ff8c6b458e",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Displacement of a Particle as a Function of Time",
      "problem": "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x\\,\\text{m},$ its acceleration is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Initially, $P$ is at the origin $O$ and has velocity $v=4\\,m/s.$ Find $x$ as a function of time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14097535/assets/ee516744d915553bf962b498af522cf0006d42c5d0e0e2a003e6f385dd069e4a.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x+1}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $x=0\\,\\text{m}$ when $v=4\\,\\text{m/s},$ we can find the value of the constant $K,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1}+K \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(4)^{2} & =8\\sqrt{0+1}+K \\\\ 8 & =8+K \\\\ K & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =8\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ {v}^{2} & =16\\sqrt{x+1} \\\\ v & =\\pm 4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing  $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can discard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we know that $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ Therefore, we have the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt[4]{x+1}.\n$$\n\nSeparating the variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\sqrt[4]{x+1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4 \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{x+1}}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}(x+1)^{3/4} & =4t+{C}_{1} \\\\ (x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=\\frac{3{C}_{1}}{4}.$ \n\nWe're given that $x=0$ when $t=0.$ Therefore, $C=1,$ and the particular solution is\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{3/4}=3t+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, writing $x$ in terms of $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{3/4} & =3t+1 \\\\ x+1 & =(3t+1)^{4/3} \\\\ x & =(3t+1)^{4/3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
